---
/**
 * THROWAWAY — a specimen page for eyeballing phase 2. Delete or replace when the
 * real page types land in phase 4.
 *
 * Its whole job is to exercise every rule in base.css against real prose so the
 * type scale, the rhythm ramp, the measure cap and both themes can be checked in
 * a browser rather than taken on trust.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Phase 2 specimen — design tokens and element role styles"
  description="Throwaway page for verifying the global tier."
>
  <div class="scaffold">
    <form class="controls">
      <label for="theme">Theme</label>
      <select id="theme" name="theme">
        <option value="system">System</option>
        <option value="light">Light</option>
        <option value="dark">Dark</option>
      </select>
      <label for="probe">Native control</label>
      <input id="probe" type="text" placeholder="checks color-scheme" />
    </form>

    <article class="detail">
      <h1>The quiet option wins</h1>

      <p>
        This page exists to be looked at. Every rule in <code>base.css</code> is exercised below, so the
        type scale, the vertical rhythm and both themes can be checked against DESIGN.md by eye. Paragraph
        text should cap at roughly sixty-seven characters — count a line if you like — while headings
        deliberately run wider, because <code>ch</code> resolves against each element's own font and a
        shared cap would mean something different for Lato than for Noto Serif.
      </p>

      <p>
        A second paragraph, to check the gap between siblings. It should be{' '}
        <strong>24px</strong>, noticeably tighter than the space above the heading that follows it.
        Inline links take the surrounding text color with a persistent underline and shift to the
        accent on hover — <a href="#nowhere">this one, for instance</a> — because the underline rather
        than the color carries the affordance. Tab to it to check the focus ring: two concentric rings,
        the inner contrasting the control and the outer the page.
      </p>

      <h2>A major heading</h2>

      <p>
        The space above that h2 should be visibly larger than the space below it — 64px against 16px
        in raw token terms, though half-leading means the <em>apparent</em> ratio is closer to 2.5:1 than
        the 4:1 the numbers suggest. Check the apparent space, not the values.
      </p>

      <h3>A minor heading</h3>

      <p>
        Above this h3, 48px. Below it, the same 16px as the h2, because{' '}
        <code>rhythm-heading-close</code> is last in source order and binds a heading to whatever follows
        it regardless of level.
      </p>

      <h4>A heading with no size step left</h4>

      <p>
        The h4 above sits at exactly the same size as this paragraph. It differentiates by family,
        weight and tracking alone — Lato 700 with 2% letter-spacing against Noto Serif 400. If it
        reads as merely bold body text, the levers are uppercase, small caps, color or a rule, never
        a smaller size.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>An unordered list, to check the 48px block gap above it.</li>
        <li>
          A second item, checking the 16px between items. This one runs long enough to wrap, so the
          leading inside an item can be compared against the gap between them.
        </li>
        <li>A third, shorter.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>And a paragraph after the list, which should also take the 48px block gap.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Ordered lists take the same treatment.</li>
        <li>Both keep the browser's own indent — DESIGN.md specifies none.</li>
      </ol>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The bar sits inside the indent, so the text starts 42px from the page edge rather than 48
          — the 6px rule plus 42px of padding. On mobile that becomes a 4px rule and 20px, and both
          come from one <code>calc()</code> so the relationship survives a change to either token.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Blockquote is an element rule rather than a component, and the indent is{' '}
        <code>padding-inline-start</code> rather than margin — a margin would put the gap outside the
        box and the bar would drift off the text block.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig-title">
          <title id="fig-title">A placeholder rectangle standing in for a figure</title>
          <rect width="640" height="300" fill="var(--color-surface-muted)"></rect>
          <rect
            x="0.5"
            y="0.5"
            width="639"
            height="299"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--color-border)"
            stroke-width="1"></rect>
          <text
            x="320"
            y="158"
            text-anchor="middle"
            fill="var(--color-text-muted)"
            style="font-family: var(--font-heading)"
            font-size="16">640 × 300</text
          >
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Captions are Noto Serif italic at the 16px step in <code>text-muted</code> — apparatus around
          the content rather than content. The figure is capped at the prose measure; the caption deliberately
          is not, because its smaller font makes <code>ch</code> resolve narrower than its own image.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Last paragraph. Switch the theme with the control at the top of the page and check all of
        the above again — every token has three places it can be wrong, and the native form controls
        should re-skin along with the page because <code>color-scheme</code> is set.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /*
   * THROWAWAY SCAFFOLDING — not the grid, and must not become it.
   *
   * base.css caps the measure but nothing centers or insets anything yet, so
   * without this the article hugs the viewport edge and the type is hard to
   * judge. The real grid — 12 columns, 996px content, 222px margins — is phase 3.
   * Delete this block when it arrives.
   */
  .scaffold {
    max-width: 62rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-3);
  }

  .controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    align-items: center;
    margin-block-end: var(--rhythm-section);
    padding-block-end: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: var(--border-width-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-1);
  }

  .controls select,
  .controls input {
    font: inherit;
    padding: var(--space-1);
    border: var(--border-width-hairline) solid var(--color-border-control);
    border-radius: var(--radius-1);
  }

  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /*
   * The toggle's wiring, unlike the resolver in BaseLayout, does NOT need to run
   * before paint — the head script has already set the theme. So this is an
   * ordinary bundled module with defer semantics, which is the cheaper default.
   *
   * "System" REMOVES both the attribute and the stored value rather than storing
   * the word "system", so following the OS is the absence of a choice rather
   * than a third state to keep in sync.
   */
  const select = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#theme')
  const root = document.documentElement

  if (select) {
    try {
      select.value = localStorage.getItem('theme') ?? 'system'
    } catch {
      select.value = 'system'
    }

    select.addEventListener('change', () => {
      const choice = select.value
      try {
        if (choice === 'system') {
          localStorage.removeItem('theme')
          delete root.dataset.theme
        } else {
          localStorage.setItem('theme', choice)
          root.dataset.theme = choice
        }
      } catch {
        // Storage blocked — apply for this page view only.
        if (choice === 'system') delete root.dataset.theme
        else root.dataset.theme = choice
      }
    })
  }
</script>
